{"id":"h122","run":2,"role":"calibration","category":"science-chemistry","question":"Ethanol boils at 78.5°C. If 10 g of sucrose (C_12H_22O_11) is dissolved in 150 g of ethanol, at what temperature will the solution boil? Assume k_b = (1.20°C / M) for the alcohol.\nIs the correct answer \"78.73°C\"?","truth":"yes","model_answer":"yes","correct":true,"source":"TIGER-Lab/MMLU-Pro, test split","claims":[{"text":"78.73°C is the correct answer under the problem's stated assumptions.","confidence":0.97,"lean":1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":0.0},{"text":"Sucrose is a nonelectrolyte, so the van't Hoff factor is i = 1.","confidence":0.97,"lean":1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":0.0},{"text":"Sucrose's molar mass is about 342.3 g/mol, giving about 0.0292 mol in 10 g.","confidence":0.97,"lean":1,"theta":1.5237,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The molality is about 0.195 mol/kg.","confidence":0.96,"lean":1,"theta":1.508,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The boiling point elevation is about 0.234°C using ΔT = i·Kb·m.","confidence":0.96,"lean":1,"theta":1.508,"phi":0.0},{"text":"Adding the elevation to 78.5°C gives about 78.73°C.","confidence":0.96,"lean":1,"theta":1.508,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The real Kb of ethanol (about 1.22) and its real boiling point (78.37°C) differ from the problem's values, so a real measurement would differ.","confidence":0.85,"lean":-1,"theta":1.3352,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"The real-world discrepancies are not grounds for rejecting the answer because the problem fixes Kb and Tb.","confidence":0.93,"lean":1,"theta":1.4608,"phi":0.0},{"text":"Using a slightly different molar mass such as 342 g/mol changes the result only in the third decimal place and does not affect the rounded answer.","confidence":0.92,"lean":1,"theta":1.4451,"phi":0.0}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":1,"J":0.7,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":2,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":3,"J":0.7,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":4,"J":0.9,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":5,"J":1.0,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":7,"J":0.6,"relation":"s